Layer 4: how objects are created, owned, copied and destroyed without exceptions, and where
their memory comes from (`memory/`).

### Acquisition, initialisation, cleanup

ca has no RAII for resources: a constructor cannot return a failure, so nothing that can fail
happens in one. An object goes through three steps, each reporting its own failure.

- Acquisition: `T::make(args...) -> result<T>` does what can fail (allocating, opening, copying),
  then builds the T with a constructor that cannot fail. `ca::make<T>(args...)` calls it; a type
  without one is built by its constructor, `T(ca::error&, args...)` when it has one (the
  constructor reports through the error and `make` returns it), else `T(args...)`. An
  allocator-aware type also has `make_in(allocator, args...)`, which `ca::make_in` calls.
- Initialisation: constructors only initialize. They acquire nothing.
- Cleanup: a type owning resources (memory, descriptors, mappings) is `ca::is_cleanable`: its
  `cleanup() -> result<void>` releases everything it owns and reports a failure, such as a close
  or a flush that failed. Its destructor remains only as a failsafe that releases silently what a
  skipped `cleanup()` left.

`make` and `make_in` are the acquisition step of every ca type, the names `ca::make` and
`ca::make_in` call: `arena::make()`, `str::make(text)`, `slice<T>::make(view)`. When acquiring
does more than build the object, a wrapper of `make` names what happens: `fs::descriptor::open(
path, options)` opens a file, `io::buffered_reader::over(fd, ...)` borrows a descriptor and
`adopt(descriptor, ...)` takes one. A factory that is not `make` has its own name when its
arguments do not say what it builds from: `path::cwd()`, `path::home()`, `hashmap::from_keys(
keys, value)` (Python's dict.fromkeys), `report::of(error)`. `fs::descriptor::adopt(fd)` and
`unique<T>::adopt(object, allocator)` take ownership of something already acquired and cannot
fail.

Moving onto a ca owner that still holds something whose release can fail (a descriptor, bytes
not yet flushed, elements with a `cleanup()`) traps, so a forgotten cleanup shows up instead of
hiding a lost error; an owner holding only memory, which frees without failing, frees it. This is
how ca's own types behave, not a requirement on yours: containers never assign over a live
element (they move-construct, and swap or reverse assign only onto an element just moved from).

```c++
#include "ca/error.hpp"
#include "ca/integers.hpp"
#include "ca/is_cleanable.hpp"
#include "ca/make.hpp"
#include "ca/result.hpp"
#include "ca/slice.hpp"
#include "ca/utility.hpp"

class counter final {
	private:
		int _limit;
		int _count;

	public:
		counter(ca::error& error, const int limit) noexcept
		: _limit{limit}, _count{0} {
			if (limit <= 0)
				error = ca::errc::invalid_argument;
		}

		[[nodiscard]] auto tick(void) noexcept -> bool {
			return ++_count <= _limit;
		}
};

class buffer final {
	private:
		ca::slice<char> _bytes;

		explicit buffer(ca::slice<char>&& bytes) noexcept
		: _bytes{ca::move(bytes)} {
		}

	public:
		[[nodiscard]] static auto make(const ca::usz size) noexcept -> ca::result<buffer> {
			auto bytes = ca::slice<char>::make(size);
			if (!bytes)
				return bytes.propagate();
			return buffer{ca::move(*bytes)};
		}

		[[nodiscard]] auto size(void) const noexcept -> ca::usz {
			return _bytes.size();
		}

		[[nodiscard]] auto cleanup(void) noexcept -> ca::result<void> {
			return _bytes.cleanup();
		}
};

static_assert(!ca::is_cleanable<counter> && ca::is_cleanable<buffer>);

auto main(void) -> int {
	auto bad = ca::make<counter>(0);
	if (bad || bad.error() != ca::errc::invalid_argument)
		return 1;
	auto made = ca::make<counter>(2);
	if (!made || !made->tick() || !made->tick() || made->tick())
		return 2;
	auto bytes = ca::make<buffer>(64U);
	if (!bytes || bytes->size() != 64U)
		return 3;
	return bytes->cleanup() ? 0 : 4;
}
```

Copies are explicit too: `ca::is_clonable` types have `clone() const -> result<T>`, a copy
that may fail (it allocates), which containers use instead of a copy constructor.

### Owning one object

`ca::unique<T, A>` owns one T in memory from allocator A (`ca::heap` by default), like
`std::unique_ptr` but built through `make` or `ca::make_in`, cleaned with `cleanup()` (which runs T's own
cleanup first), and cloned with `clone()`. Over a stateless allocator it is one pointer wide.

`ca::make_in<T>(source, args...)` builds an allocator-aware T over any allocator or allocator
resource: `ca::make_in<ca::unique<T>>(ca::heap{}, ...)` on the heap, or `ca::make_in<
ca::dynamic_array<int>>(arena)` in an arena, the type rebound to that allocator.

```c++
#include "ca/make_in.hpp"
#include "ca/memory.hpp"
#include "ca/unique.hpp"
#include "ca/utility.hpp"

struct node final {
	int value;
	explicit node(const int v) noexcept : value{v} {
	}
};

auto main(void) -> int {
	auto made = ca::make_in<ca::unique<node>>(ca::heap{}, 7);
	if (!made || (*made)->value != 7)
		return 1;
	ca::unique<node> owner = ca::move(*made);
	auto copy = owner.clone();
	if (!copy || (*copy)->value != 7 || !copy->cleanup())
		return 2;

	auto arena = ca::arena::make();
	if (!arena)
		return 3;
	auto in_arena = ca::make_in<ca::unique<node>>(*arena, 9);
	if (!in_arena || (*in_arena)->value != 9 || !in_arena->cleanup())
		return 4;
	return owner.cleanup() && arena->cleanup() ? 0 : 5;
}
```

### Contexts

A context is Python's `with` statement for a world without exceptions: setup, a body, and a
leave step that always runs, told whether the body failed. Every `ca::trap` reached inside the
body (an out-of-range index, a failed result read, an explicit trap) unwinds to the context, which
fails with that error instead of ending the program.

`ca::with(enter, leave, args...)` builds one from two callables; `ca::context_manager<Self>` is
the CRTP base for a reusable context class with `enter(args...)` and `leave(const E* error)`,
run by `self.run(args...)` or `ca::ctx<Self>::run(args...)`.

```c++
#include "ca/context_manager.hpp"
#include "ca/dynamic_array.hpp"
#include "ca/error.hpp"
#include "ca/with.hpp"

struct job final : ca::context_manager<job> {
	int left = 0;
	bool failed = false;

	auto enter(ca::dynamic_array<int>& values, const ca::usz index) noexcept -> void {
		values[index] = 1;
	}

	auto leave(const ca::error* const error) noexcept -> void {
		++left;
		failed = error != nullptr;
	}
};

auto main(void) -> int {
	ca::dynamic_array<int> values{};
	if (!values.append(0))
		return 1;
	job j{};
	if (!j.run(values, 0U) || j.failed)
		return 2;
	auto out_of_range = j.run(values, 5U);
	if (out_of_range || out_of_range.error() != ca::errc::not_recoverable || !j.failed || j.left != 2)
		return 3;

	int entered = 0;
	auto done = ca::with([&entered](const int n) noexcept { entered = n; },
		[](const ca::error*) noexcept {}, 3);
	return done && entered == 3 && values.cleanup() ? 0 : 4;
}
```

A trap skips the destructors of the frames it unwinds: what they owned leaks, so `leave` is
where a context releases what `enter` acquired. Inside a context, reading a failed result fails
the context with that result's error, so a body can be written as straight-line code with `*r`
and still report the first error that happened.

### Which context takes a trap

A trap runs in two phases. First, at the trap site and before anything unwinds, each context from
the innermost outward decides on it through `handles(const ca::trapped&) -> ca::verdict`: `take`
it, `pass` it to an outer context, or `resume` the code (`ca::ask` only). Then every context
passed runs its `leave`, innermost first, and the trap unwinds to the one that took it. A context
without `handles` takes every trap, as before. No context taking it stops the program right at
the trap, with every frame intact.

| Piece | Role |
|---|---|
| `handles(const ca::trapped&)` | optional: decides at the trap site; `ca::handler{f}` for `ca::with` |
| `ca::trapped` | the trap: `reason()`, `where()`, `error()`, `as<E>()`, `resumable()`, `backtrace()` |
| `ca::verdict` | `take`, `pass`, `resume` |
| `leave(error, ca::outcome)` | optional second parameter: `succeeded`, `taken` or `passed` |
| `ca::ask(reason, error)` | a trap the caller can come back from: true when a context resumed it |

A context passed over still runs `leave`, with outcome `passed`, and its `run` never returns: the
trap goes on to the outer context, so `leave` must release everything. `handles`, and `leave` when
passed, run while the trap is in flight and must not trap: that stops the program.

`ca::ask` is for code written to carry on: it returns true when a context resumed it (it fixed the
cause, check again) and false when none took it (fail the usual way). Contexts without `handles`
pass it; one that answers `take` unwinds there as for a trap.

```c++
#include "ca/ask.hpp"
#include "ca/context_manager.hpp"
#include "ca/debug.hpp"
#include "ca/error.hpp"
#include "ca/handler.hpp"
#include "ca/outcome.hpp"
#include "ca/trapped.hpp"
#include "ca/with.hpp"

struct pool final {
	int free;
	int spare;
};

static auto take(pool& from) noexcept -> ca::result<void> {
	while (from.free == 0) {
		if (!ca::ask("pool empty", ca::errc::out_of_memory))
			return ca::failure{ca::errc::out_of_memory};
	}
	--from.free;
	return {};
}

struct refill final : ca::context_manager<refill> {
	pool* from = nullptr;
	ca::outcome ended = ca::outcome::succeeded;

	auto handles(const ca::trapped& trap) noexcept -> ca::verdict {
		if (!trap.resumable())
			return ca::verdict::pass;
		if (this->from->spare == 0)
			return ca::verdict::pass;
		--this->from->spare;
		++this->from->free;
		return ca::verdict::resume;
	}

	auto enter(pool& p, const int count) noexcept -> void {
		this->from = &p;
		for (int i = 0; i < count; ++i)
			take(p).raise();
	}

	auto leave(const ca::error*, const ca::outcome outcome) noexcept -> void {
		this->ended = outcome;
	}
};

auto main(void) -> int {
	pool p{1, 1};
	refill r{};
	if (!r.run(p, 2) || p.spare != 0 || r.ended != ca::outcome::succeeded)
		return 1;

	auto outer = ca::with(
		[&](void) noexcept {
			static_cast<void>(r.run(p, 1));
		},
		[](const ca::error*) noexcept {});
	if (outer || outer.error() != ca::errc::out_of_memory || r.ended != ca::outcome::passed)
		return 2;

	auto only_mine = ca::with(
		ca::handler{[](const ca::trapped& trap) noexcept {
			return trap.reason() == ca::sv::str{"mine"} ? ca::verdict::take : ca::verdict::pass;
		}},
		[](void) noexcept { ca::trap("mine"); },
		[](const ca::error*) noexcept {});
	return only_mine ? 3 : 0;
}
```

In the second run the pool is empty and `refill` has no spare: it passes the `ask`, `take` returns
its failure, `raise` traps, `refill` passes that trap too, and the outer context takes it, after
`refill`'s `leave` saw `passed`.
